      "writtenOn": "2025-09-18T07:57:40Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "We have observed that there is a certain delay between a process being killed and the killed process being unfrozen in our product (sometimes reaching several hundred milliseconds). This delay may be caused by slow binder calls, slow message passing, scheduling latency, etc. By directly unfreezing the killed process at the time of killing, this delay can be avoided.\n\nIn addition, if only a child process of an application is killed, unfreezing based on the freezer cgroup often means that extra processes that are not being killed will also be unfrozen. This can cause those non-killed processes to become active on the CPU again, increasing the load. Directly unfreezing only the killed process can avoid this unnecessary unfreezing.",
      "parentUuid": "6ec0daf4_e70668e9",
      "range": {
